14 0 obj
<</Parent 18 0 R/Next 15 0 R/Prev 12 0 R/First 13 0 R/Last 13 0 R/Count -1/Title(Interpretatiion of the above plot: The chart shows that Caste 3 has the highest percentage in all regions, with the strongest presence in the South. Caste 2 and Caste 4 appear in moderate proportions across every zone. Caste 1 and Caste 6 have very low representation everywhere. The North region looks more balanced compared to others, with several castes having similar values. In contrast, the West and South are more dominated by one major caste group. The East and Central regions show a mixed pattern without extreme dominance. Overall, caste distribution is uneven and changes from one geographical zone to another.)/Dest 1003 0 R>>
endobj
15 0 obj
<</Parent 18 0 R/Next 17 0 R/Prev 14 0 R/Title(PLOT 2 : Density plot the Distribution of per capita income across zone and remove outliers from income)/Dest 1005 0 R>>
endobj
16 0 obj
<</Parent 17 0 R/Title(Final Check)/Dest 1007 0 R>>
endobj
17 0 obj
<</Parent 18 0 R/Prev 15 0 R/First 16 0 R/Last 16 0 R/Count -1/Title(Interpretation of the above plot: The density plot shows that most people in all regions fall in the lower income range, as the curves peak at lower values. The East and Central regions have slightly higher peaks, indicating more people concentrated at lower incomes. The North and South regions show a wider spread, suggesting more variation in income levels. The West also follows a similar pattern but with a slightly smoother distribution. All regions have a long right tail, meaning a smaller number of people earn much higher incomes. Overall, income distribution is positively skewed across all geographical zones.)/Dest 1006 0 R>>
endobj
